WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.440
Moviegoers of all ages probably&nbsp;
recognize Joe Pesci from his streetwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.520
fast-talking performances in films&nbsp;
like "Goodfellas" and "Home Alone."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.200
Pesci has stayed mostly out of the&nbsp;
spotlight since the turn of the millennium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.000
but that doesn't mean he's disappeared&nbsp;
entirely. Here is the untold truth of Joe Pesci.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.360
Born Joseph Frank Pesci on February 9, 1943 in&nbsp;
Newark, New Jersey, Joe Pesci was thrown into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.800
spotlight at an early age by his father, Angelo,&nbsp;
who saw show business as a ticket to giving his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.760
family a better life. By the time he was five&nbsp;
years old, Pesci made his debut on "Star Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:43.120
Kids," a television variety series that aired from&nbsp;
1950 to 1955. The experience was, by Pesci's own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.560
description, less than pleasurable: in a 1992&nbsp;
interview with The New York Times, he said,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.040
"My father loved me so much that he did&nbsp;
not want me to be a laborer or anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.480
I don't know if it's the right thing to&nbsp;
do — push your kids into something and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.520
then stay on them until they do it.&nbsp;
Let them pick what they want to do."

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.560
Though Pesci was less than enthused about&nbsp;
performing, it kept him afloat during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.240
pre-fame days. Pesci sang and played guitar in a&nbsp;
string of pop and rock acts, most notably in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.680
touring version of the popular New York act Joey&nbsp;
Dee and the Starliters, which provided him with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.880
his first feature film appearance as an extra&nbsp;
in the band's star vehicle, "Hey, Let's Twist!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.640
Pesci then made his bid for pop stardom in&nbsp;
1968 with the release of his first solo album,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.240
"Little Joe Sure Can Sing!" But the LP wasn't a&nbsp;
hit, so he returned to gigging alongside another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.920
Jersey musician, Frank Vincent. However, live club&nbsp;
music had fallen out of fashion by the early '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.600
so the pair turned to comedy,&nbsp;
forming the duo Vincent and Pesci.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.560
"I'm funny how, you mean I'm funny like a&nbsp;
clown, like I amuse you? I make you laugh?"

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.160
Decades after they hung up the act,&nbsp;
Vincent would remain Pesci's friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.760
off-camera and frequent nemesis on film: Pesci&nbsp;
brutalized Vincent in "Raging Bull" and beat him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.880
to death in "Goodfellas," while Vincent returned&nbsp;
the favor by bashing in Pesci's skull in "Casino."

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.760
In 1976, Pesci had his first credited movie role&nbsp;
in "Family Enforcer," also known as "The Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.280
Collector," a low-budget Mafia thriller starring&nbsp;
Pesci and Joe Cortese as muscle for the Mob.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.880
Solid reviews prompted Pesci to head&nbsp;
to Hollywood, but a lack of further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.120
success sent him packing back to the Bronx,&nbsp;
where he worked as a restaurant manager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.760
It was at this low point that Martin&nbsp;
Scorsese and Robert De Niro came calling;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.160
the director and star had seen Pesci in "Family&nbsp;
Enforcer" and sought him out for their next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.520
project, the boxing drama "Raging Bull." Pesci&nbsp;
played Joey LaMotta, the brother, manager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.560
and eventual enemy of short-tempered boxer Jake&nbsp;
LaMotta, which earned him a nomination for Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.880
Supporting Actor at the Oscars the following&nbsp;
year and made his name one to remember.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.720
From 1980 to 1998, Pesci lent his unique&nbsp;
charm to a handful of iconic movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.920
from Scorcese collaborations such as "Goodfellas''&nbsp;
and "Casino" to comedies like "Home Alone" and "My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.840
Cousin Vinny." He had a small part opposite De&nbsp;
Niro in the legendary Sergio Leone's last film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.480
"Once Upon a Time in America," portrayed&nbsp;
real-life pilot David Ferrie in "JFK,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.160
and had a recurring role as Leo Getz&nbsp;
in the "Lethal Weapon" franchise.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.400
However, many of Pesci's credits are likely&nbsp;
to be total obscurities to even the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.040
seasoned moviegoers. For instance,&nbsp;
in 1983, Pesci starred as a bowling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.600
alley owner with dreams of stardom in the&nbsp;
West German musical "Dear Mr. Wonderful."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.840
It's also likely that you missed Pesci as&nbsp;
the uncle of the bride in "Betsy's Wedding,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.840
or his thinly veiled take on legendary&nbsp;
photographer Weegee in "The Public Eye."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.840
The takeaway? Pesci's hits are very big, but&nbsp;
his misses disappear, with little in between.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.960
Pesci has given few interviews since announcing&nbsp;
his retirement, so his reasons for the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.600
remain solely with him. However, it's possible&nbsp;
that he simply lost his taste for the projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.560
that were being offered to him. As far back&nbsp;
as 1992, Pesci told The New York Times that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.280
many of the roles brought to him were, quote,&nbsp;
"stupid, ethnic Italian parts [with] pizza-pie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.920
stupid jokes." While he mostly avoided that&nbsp;
characterization in his film work, Pesci did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.040
withstand a barrage of jokes about his height&nbsp;
in the dismal NBC action-comedy "Half Nelson."

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.560
Pesci was top-billed as Rocky Nelson, an NYPD&nbsp;
detective who works for a private security firm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.960
while trying to launch an acting career.&nbsp;
The series, co-created by Glen A. Larson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.080
paired Pesci with "Saturday Night Live" alum&nbsp;
Victoria Jackson as a scatterbrained secretary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:28.160
former football player Fred Williamson as Rocky's&nbsp;
boss, and a visibly unwell Dean Martin as himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.720
At the time of its release, critic David&nbsp;
Handler described "Half Nelson" as, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.920
"[managing] to leave you&nbsp;
feeling both bored and soiled,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.000
which undoubtedly helped capsize the&nbsp;
series after just seven episodes.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.360
Joe Pesci's Oscar win for his portrayal of&nbsp;
gangster Tommy DeVito in "Goodfellas" helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.640
to wipe away any residue from "Half Nelson" or his&nbsp;
other early obscurities, and it's likely that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.360
saw his win as the start of a new and improved&nbsp;
phase of his career. He told The New York Times,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.040
"I love to star in movies, but I want&nbsp;
to have good roles. It doesn't help to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.120
get starring roles in something that's no&nbsp;
good. I mean, that will just kill you."

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.240
Although he netted several box office hits, much&nbsp;
of his output during the 1990s was dismissed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.440
critics and audiences alike. Attempts to fashion&nbsp;
Pesci into a comedic leading man in "The Super"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.480
and "Gone Fishin'" were busts, as was his attempt&nbsp;
at tugging heartstrings in the terminally soggy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.240
"With Honors," which cast him as a homeless man&nbsp;
with a big heart and a scholar's brain. By 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.240
Pesci had clearly lost his patience with&nbsp;
the industry: after logging his third and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.040
final turn as machine gun-mouthed Leo Getz in&nbsp;
"Lethal Weapon 4" — which earned him a Razzie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.840
nomination — he retired from acting as a full-time&nbsp;
profession and focused his energies elsewhere.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.379
"I'm sorry, okay, okay, okay,&nbsp;
okay! Whatever, whatever!"

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:45.200
With several notable exceptions, Pesci mostly&nbsp;
stuck to his decision to put acting on the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.440
burner. Instead, he focused his energy&nbsp;
on golf and, to the surprise of many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.200
went back to singing. In 1998, Pesci released&nbsp;
his second album, "Vincent LaGuardia Gambini&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.080
Sings Just for You," named for his character&nbsp;
in "My Cousin Vinny." Despite his ill-advised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.800
pass at hip-hop with the infamous single "Wise&nbsp;
Guy," Pesci still proved his musical prowess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.440
with renditions of "What a Wonderful World" and&nbsp;
the Ray Charles number "I've Got News For You."

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.080
Although critics and listeners alike largely&nbsp;
dismissed the album as a vanity project, Pesci&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.440
kept his hand in music. As "Joe Doggs," he earned&nbsp;
respectable reviews for "Falling in Love Again," a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.120
collaboration with jazz organist Joey DeFrancesco,&nbsp;
and held his own alongside his onetime idol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.840
Little Jimmy Scott, on "The Folks Who Live on&nbsp;
the Hill," a duet featured on the legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:35.520
vocalist's final studio album, "I Go Back Home."&nbsp;
Two years later, Pesci released his third album,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.680
"Still Singing," which featured two&nbsp;
duets with Maroon 5's Adam Levine.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.120
In a 2016 interview, comedy legend Don Rickles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.080
who appeared with Pesci in&nbsp;
"Casino," said of his co-star,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.920
"I think sometimes he believed he was&nbsp;
with the Mob. But he got over it."

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.720
Given Pesci's multitude of terrifying gangster&nbsp;
performances, it's understandable how some might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.880
misconstrue him as a made man in the real&nbsp;
world. While there are tales of the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.920
interacting with career criminals, Pesci has kept&nbsp;
himself out of trouble offscreen. Well, mostly.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.280
In 2006, Pesci was involved in an alleged&nbsp;
assault against an overzealous fan in Florida.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.840
According to a police report, Pesci struck a man&nbsp;
who ignored his request to refrain from taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.480
his picture, but prosecutors dismissed&nbsp;
the allegations over a lack of evidence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.200
A half-decade later, Pesci was involved in&nbsp;
not one, but two legal cases: the first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.920
in 2012, involved his ex-wife, actress&nbsp;
Claudia Haro, who was sentenced to 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:33.040
years for allegedly hiring a hitman to whack&nbsp;
her second husband, stuntman Garrett Warren.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:37.200
Pesci was briefly considered as the supposed&nbsp;
source of financing for the failed hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.760
though the allegations were eventually dismissed.&nbsp;
The following year, Pesci was awarded an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.200
unspecified court settlement from Fiore Pictures&nbsp;
after he sued the company for allegedly reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.640
his salary and role in what would become the&nbsp;
infamous 2018 biopic "Gotti" with John Travolta.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.560
There's no question that Joe&nbsp;
Pesci is a complicated individual.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.760
Even he's noted as much: in an&nbsp;
interview with the Baltimore Sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.680
he recalled having an existential crisis&nbsp;
while playing a game of golf, saying:

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.280
"I didn't know who the hell was about&nbsp;
to hit that golf ball. Was it Leo Getz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.160
or David Ferrie or Tommy or Harry or Joe?&nbsp;
I've spent so much time as somebody else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.080
and so little time as myself, I lost&nbsp;
sight of who I was for an instant."

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.800
Pesci is an enormously talented actor, and&nbsp;
if his quietly menacing performance in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:26.240
Irishman" is any indication, one still capable&nbsp;
of mesmerizing audiences well into his 70s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.920
However, he seems to prefer to focus&nbsp;
on other outlets that appear to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.400
greater hold on his heart, including singing&nbsp;
and tending to his own personal well-being.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.600
If acting has left him feeling&nbsp;
misunderstood, misrepresented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.000
and even unsure of who he really is, it's hard&nbsp;
to blame him for choosing to step away from it.

